.Dd October 20, 2026
.Dt PAZARI 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pazari
.Nd guest-initiated memory negotiation for virtual machines
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Virtual machines are usually given a fixed amount of memory, chosen before
anyone knows what the workload will need.
.Nm
lets the guest ask for memory as it needs it and return it when it is done.
The guest can tell a workload that is growing apart from one that is thrashing;
the host cannot.
.Pp
.Nm
is Greek for haggling.
.Pp
It has three programs:
.Bl -tag -width "pazari-stress"
.It Xr pazari-agent 1
Runs in the guest.
Throttles one workload cgroup before it runs out of memory, and asks the host
for more.
.It Xr pazarid 1
Runs on the host.
Decides which guests may grow and resizes their virtio-mem devices over QMP.
.It Xr pazari-stress 1
Generates predictable memory load for testing.
.El
.Pp
The agent and the daemon exchange newline-delimited JSON over vsock or a unix
socket.
Every message carries absolute values, and neither side keeps state across a
connection.
A side that receives a message it does not understand closes the connection,
and the guest reconnects.
.Sh HOW IT WORKS
A grow proceeds as follows:
.Bl -enum
.It
The workload reaches
.Va memory.high ,
and the kernel throttles it.
.It
The agent asks the daemon for more memory.
The workload continues, throttled, into the burst reserve below
.Va memory.max .
.It
The daemon grants the request if the host can spare the memory, and enlarges
the guest's virtio-mem device.
.It
The guest kernel onlines the new memory, and the agent raises the workload's
limits to match.
.El
.Pp
When the guest has had memory to spare for a while, the agent reclaims it from
the workload and offers it back.
The host can ask for memory back under pressure, but cannot take it.
.Sh DEPLOYMENT
.Ss Memory device
Give the guest a virtio-mem device.
With QEMU:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
qemu-system-x86_64 \e
    -machine q35 \e
    -m 2048M,maxmem=32768M \e
    -object memory-backend-ram,id=vmem0,size=30720M \e
    -device virtio-mem-pci,id=vm0,memdev=vmem0,requested-size=0 \e
    ...
.Ed
.Pp
The backend's
.Va size
is the most the guest can grow by.
.Va maxmem
must be at least boot memory plus that.
The device's
.Va id
is what
.Xr pazarid.toml 5
refers to.
The machine type must have PCI;
.Va microvm
does not support virtio-mem.
.Pp
Leave the device's block size at its default.
Larger blocks bring no measurable benefit, and a block size above the guest
kernel's memory block size, 128MiB on x86-64, causes resizes to be refused.
.Ss QMP socket
Give the daemon its own QMP socket, rather than sharing the one your VM manager
uses:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
-qmp unix:/run/pazari/example.qmp,server=on,wait=off
.Ed
.Pp
QMP gives full control of the hypervisor, so a separate socket keeps the
daemon's access easy to audit and revoke.
Run the daemon as its own user, in the socket's group.
.Pp
For vsock, load
.Va vhost_vsock
on the host.
.Ss Guest kernel
Boot the guest with:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
transparent_hugepage=madvise psi=1 memhp_default_state=online_movable
.Ed
.Pp
The last parameter keeps kernel allocations out of plugged memory, which could
otherwise never be fully unplugged.
All kernel memory must then fit in boot memory, and it grows with plugged
memory and with the number of cached files.
Size boot memory for the most the guest can grow by, and cap
.Va vm.dirty_bytes .
.Pp
The kernel patches under
.Pa nix/patches
in the source tree remove most of that cost.
.Pp
Do not give the guest a virtio-balloon device.
Run
.Ql pazari-agent preflight
to check the guest before deploying.
.Ss Units
Put the workload in its own slice, and exclude it from
.Xr systemd-oomd 8 ,
which would otherwise treat a deliberately throttled workload as one in trouble:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# workload.slice
[Slice]
MemoryAccounting=yes
ManagedOOMMemoryPressure=auto
ManagedOOMSwap=auto
ManagedOOMPreference=omit
.Ed
.Pp
.Va ManagedOOMPreference=omit
excludes the slice.
The other two settings prevent it opting in.
.Pp
Run the agent outside that slice:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# pazari-agent.service
[Service]
Type=notify
ExecStart=/usr/bin/pazari-agent
ExecStopPost=/usr/bin/pazari-agent panic-open
WatchdogSec=30s
Restart=always
RestartSec=5s
Slice=system.slice
.Ed
.Pp
The
.Va ExecStopPost
line releases the throttle if the agent is killed.
Without it, the workload stays throttled until the agent is restarted.
.Pp
On the host:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# pazarid.service
[Service]
Type=notify
ExecStart=/usr/bin/pazarid
WatchdogSec=30s
Restart=always
RestartSec=5s
User=pazarid
SupplementaryGroups=kvm
.Ed
.Ss Configuration
Generate a starting configuration for each program and edit it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# pazarid example-config > /etc/pazari/pazarid.toml
# pazari-agent example-config > /etc/pazari/agent.toml
.Ed
.Pp
See
.Xr pazarid.toml 5
and
.Xr pazari-agent.toml 5 .
.Sh CONTAINERS
The agent only sees memory charged to its cgroup, so the workload must run
inside it.
.Pp
A container run from a Quadlet unit follows its unit: set
.Va Slice=workload.slice
on the unit.
.Pp
Containers created through a Docker or Podman socket, as CI runners do, are
placed by that daemon instead.
Docker on cgroup v2 puts them in
.Pa system.slice ,
where the agent cannot see them.
Set
.Va cgroup-parent
in
.Pa /etc/docker/daemon.json
to
.Ql workload.slice ,
or the equivalent for your container engine.
.Pp
Keep the container daemon itself outside the slice, and count its memory in
.Cm service_reserve .
.Sh MONITORING
On a healthy host, watch:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Va pazarid_host_available_bytes
Against
.Cm grant.floor .
.It Va pazarid_denies_total
By reason.
.It Va pazari_agent_mode
How long guests spend in
.Cm growing .
.It Va pazari_agent_max_events
Should stay at 0.
An exhaustion that releases the brake is the exception; see
.Xr pazari-agent 1 .
Anything else means a workload reached
.Va memory.max
despite the brake.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /etc/pazari/agent.toml
Agent configuration.
.It Pa /etc/pazari/pazarid.toml
Daemon configuration.
.It Pa share/doc/pazari/examples
Annotated examples of both.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pazari-agent 1 ,
.Xr pazari-stress 1 ,
.Xr pazarid 1 ,
.Xr pazari-agent.toml 5 ,
.Xr pazarid.toml 5 ,
.Xr cgroups 7 ,
.Xr systemd-oomd 8
